# Back Orifice (1998). A remote-control tool: whoever ran it handed their PC to a stranger on the internet.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee files crew timesheets, does one safely, then runs a "funny game" a friend mailed and
# finds out what the program with the blank name was doing. Dana's job is invented: Back Orifice's victims were ordinary
# Windows 95 and 98 users, at home and at work, who ran a game or a greeting program someone sent them.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | Back Orifice was glued to a harmless-looking program, such as a game, and mailed around. The game ran normally while a hidden remote-control tool installed itself.
  | Once it was running, a stranger could use the PC from the internet: open and read files, watch the screen, log every key typed, pop up messages and start programs. Its makers, a hacker group, released it in 1998 as a "joke" to show how weak Windows 98 was.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, office coordinator at Northwind Traders. At the start of each week you key last week's warehouse hours into the company payroll portal, one timesheet per crew.
  | It is Monday morning, 2 November. You have filed 8 of last week's 10 timesheets. Two to go.
  | Your sign-in details and the crews' bank details sit in text files on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 8 10 Timesheets filed this week

# Dana's desk: soft flags (careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/payroll-login.txt
  at 300 480
  | Northwind Payroll Portal
  | Staff ID: dana.okafor
  | Password: Crew-Hours-26
file Desktop/crew-bank-details.txt
  at 430 480
  | Warehouse crew bank details (training copy)
  | Amir Haddad   00-00-00   12345678
  | Lena Brandt   00-00-00   87654321
  | Tomas Silva   00-00-00   55555555

# The payroll portal: a sign-in page, and the timesheet pages behind it.
page pay-login payroll.northwind.example/sign-in
  title Northwind Payroll: sign in
  brand Northwind Payroll
  favorite
  heading Sign in to the Payroll Portal
  | Welcome back. Sign in to file your crew timesheets.
  field Staff ID = dana.okafor
  field secret Password = Crew-Hours-26
  button Sign in
page filed-9 payroll.northwind.example/timesheets/done
  title Timesheet filed
  brand Northwind Payroll
  heading Timesheet filed
  | Warehouse crew 9: 212 hours for the week ending 30 October 2026 are with Payroll.
page back-9 payroll.northwind.example/timesheets/returned
  title Timesheet sent back
  brand Northwind Payroll
  heading Timesheet sent back
  | Warehouse crew 9 goes back to its supervisor to be checked.
page ts-9 payroll.northwind.example/timesheets/crew-9
  title File timesheet: Warehouse crew 9
  brand Northwind Payroll
  requires pay-login
  heading File timesheet: Warehouse crew 9
  | A crew timesheet is waiting to be filed.
  section Timesheet
  row Crew = Warehouse crew 9
  row Week ending = 30 October 2026
  row Total hours = 212
  row Employees = 6
  button File timesheet -> filed-9
  button Send back to supervisor -> back-9

mail mail-ts9
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Timesheet to file: Warehouse crew 9
  link ts-9
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | Crew 9's hours are ready in the portal. The link goes straight to the timesheet.
  |
  | Priya, Payroll

step Timesheet 9 of 10
  look Mail: Priya's message with the timesheet link
  | A new timesheet has come in from Priya, in Payroll. This one is real. File it the normal way, so you know what normal looks like.
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Timesheet to file: Warehouse crew 9

step The link goes to the portal
  look the link under Priya's message
  when mail mail-ts9
  | The email links to the company's own address, payroll.northwind.example. Hover over a link before you click it, to check where it goes.
open mail-ts9

step Sign in as usual
  look the two boxes: Staff ID and Password, as on every visit
  when visit ts-9
  move Copy the details from payroll-login.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The portal asks you to sign in first. Two boxes: Staff ID and Password. Remember how quiet the screen is while you type.
open ts-9

step Check, then file
  look the crew, the week and the hours
  when choose pay-login 1
  | You are in. Check the crew and the hours, then file the timesheet or send it back.
signin pay-login

# The lure: a game from a friend, with the remote-control tool joined to it. Windows hides the .exe, so it reads whack-the-mole.
step 9 of 10 done
  look the counter in the guide: one timesheet left
  when choose ts-9 any
  | Done. One timesheet to go. And a personal email has just arrived at your work address.
choose ts-9 1
quota +1
view bo-game panel
  title Whack-the-Mole
  app Whack-the-Mole
  size 520x360
  icon program
  heading Whack-the-Mole
  counter Score: {n} 0 12
  | Click the moles as fast as you can. Press Quit when you have had enough.
  button Quit -> close primary
view bo-msg message
  title Hello
  icon info
  | Hello from your new administrator. I can see your files, your screen and what you type. (Fictional message from the remote controller.)
  button OK -> done primary
program whack-the-mole whack-the-mole
  publisher none
  signed no
  prompt open-warning
  description Funny game
  process ⠀.exe
  cpu 3
  mem 1
  net UDP 0.0.0.0:31337 (listening for commands)
  keylog yes
  parent explorer.exe
  command C:\Windows\System\ .exe
  startup ⠀.exe C:\Windows\System\ .exe
  location HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunServices
  view bo-game
mail game
  from Sam Patel <sam.patel@mailbox.example>
  subject Funny game, try it
  attach Downloads/whack-the-mole.exe
  | Dana, my cousin wrote this thing and it is hilarious. Whack-the-Mole, takes 2 minutes. Try it on your break!
  |
  | Sam
file Downloads/whack-the-mole.exe
  program whack-the-mole
  size 233472
notify mail Sam Patel
  | Funny game, try it

step A game from a friend
  look the sender, and the attachment: whack-the-mole, with no document in sight
  when mail game
  | From Sam, a friend, at a personal address, with a program attached instead of a link. Windows hides the end of its name: it is really whack-the-mole.exe, a program. Sam's own PC may already be infected, which is how it reached you. Flag anything that looks wrong.
open game

step Windows asks before it runs
  look Publisher "Unknown Publisher", Type "Application"
  when prompt whack-the-mole
  | A game from a friend still asks whether to run a program from an unknown publisher. Most victims clicked Run.
open Downloads/whack-the-mole.exe

step The game works fine
  look the Whack-the-Mole window: it plays normally. And the firewall alert and the notice that appeared with it
  when run whack-the-mole
  move Flag the alert if it looks wrong and answer it, then press the chip “1 program running” in the guide to open {Task Manager|Activity Monitor}.
  cue dialog-btn:ok app:taskmanager
  | The game really does run, so nothing looks wrong. That is the trick: it also installed a second program, which copied itself into the system folder and now starts every time the computer starts.
  | It has also just opened port 31337 and is waiting for a stranger to connect. A game has no reason to accept connections from the internet.
answer run
dialog firewall Downloads/whack-the-mole.exe

# Priya's last timesheet arrives; the portal signs Dana out, as it does after a while.
page filed-10 payroll.northwind.example/timesheets/done
  title Timesheet filed
  brand Northwind Payroll
  heading Timesheet filed
  | Warehouse crew 10: 188 hours for the week ending 30 October 2026 are with Payroll.
page back-10 payroll.northwind.example/timesheets/returned
  title Timesheet sent back
  brand Northwind Payroll
  heading Timesheet sent back
  | Warehouse crew 10 goes back to its supervisor to be checked.
page ts-10 payroll.northwind.example/timesheets/crew-10
  title File timesheet: Warehouse crew 10
  brand Northwind Payroll
  requires pay-login
  heading File timesheet: Warehouse crew 10
  | A crew timesheet is waiting to be filed.
  section Timesheet
  row Crew = Warehouse crew 10
  row Week ending = 30 October 2026
  row Total hours = 188
  row Employees = 5
  button File timesheet -> filed-10
  button Send back to supervisor -> back-10
signout pay-login
mail mail-ts10
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Last one of the week: Warehouse crew 10
  link ts-10
  | Dana,
  |
  | Crew 10 is the last timesheet. Same as before: the link opens it in the portal.
  |
  | Priya

step A program with a blank name
  look Task Manager (Activity Monitor on a Mac): a process whose name starts with a blank, and the same name in the start-up list
  when open taskmanager
  move Look through the list for a name that starts with a blank. Then open Priya's new email from Mail.
  | Back Orifice copied itself into the system folder under a name that looks blank, so it hides in a list. It also added itself to the start-up list, so it comes back every time the computer starts.
  | It is now listening on port 31337 for a stranger's commands, and it records every key. Flag what does not belong. Priya's last timesheet has just arrived.
answer ok
open taskmanager
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Last one of the week: Warehouse crew 10

step The last timesheet
  look Mail: Priya's last message
  when mail mail-ts10
  | Priya's last timesheet of the week, with a company link like the first one. Open it.
open mail-ts10

step Signed out
  look the sign-in page: the same as before, but now something is reading every key
  when visit ts-10
  move Sign in again with the details from the desktop file. Watch the network icon while you type.
  | The portal signed you out, so sign in again. The page is exactly the page you used earlier: nothing on it has changed.
open ts-10

step Someone else is at the controls
  look the message box on top of the page, and the network icon
  when choose pay-login 1
  move Read the message box that has appeared, then press OK.
  | You are signed in. Your Staff ID and password went to the stranger as you typed them. And a message box has appeared that you did not open: someone on the internet is using your PC.
signin pay-login
show bo-msg

step Programs open by themselves
  look the Calculator: nobody opened it
  when done bo-msg
  | The stranger can start programs on your PC, read your files, watch your screen and log your keys, and from your side of the screen it looks like ghosts. Close the Calculator whenever you like.
open calculator

step 10 of 10, or so Dana thinks
  look the counter: the shift is done
  when choose ts-10 any
  | Every timesheet is filed, and the work looks normal. Press Next to see what the stranger saw.
choose ts-10 1
quota +1

step What the stranger saw
  look the crew bank details that just opened, and the animation: everything you typed after the game ran
  | The stranger has the portal password, and opened the crews' bank details on your desktop to read them. Back Orifice gave them everything Dana could do on her PC, past one start-up notice and one firewall alert most people click away.
open Desktop/crew-bank-details.txt
attack spyware-surveillance
  | {captured}
  | the crew bank details
  | the screen, live
  | message boxes

flag hard mail game
  | A game nobody asked for, with a program attached, from a personal address. A friend's own PC may already be infected.
flag hard file Downloads/whack-the-mole.exe
  | A program sent by email, with no known author: running it gives it everything you can do on this PC. Windows hides the .exe at the end of its name.
flag hard dialog open-warning whack-the-mole
  | A game from a friend still asks to run as a program. Publisher: Unknown.
flag hard dialog firewall whack-the-mole
  | A game has no reason to accept connections from the internet: Back Orifice is opening port 31337 for a stranger.
flag hard process ⠀.exe
  label The process with the blank name
  | Back Orifice running in the background under a name that looks blank, so it hides in the list.
flag hard startup ⠀.exe
  label The start-up entry with the blank name
  | Back Orifice added itself to the programs that start every time Windows does.
flag hard notice settings New startup item
  | Windows said a program added itself to start-up when the game ran. A game has no reason to start with the PC.
flag hard view bo-msg
  | A message box you never opened, from someone who says they can see your screen: a stranger is controlling this PC.
flag hard net
  | Every key you type is recorded for the stranger, who collects the log over the open port.
flag soft file Desktop/payroll-login.txt
  | Passwords in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs, or any stranger at the controls, can read them.
flag soft file Desktop/crew-bank-details.txt
  | Other people's bank details belong in the payroll system, not in a file on the desktop.

lesson
  | Do not run programs sent by email, even from friends: their account or PC may already be infected. A game that nobody asked for is the oldest way in.
  | A game is a program too: Properties (Get Info on a Mac) shows its kind as Application, and an Unknown Publisher warning means nobody vouches for it. A program with a blank or odd name in Task Manager is worth asking IT about.
  | A personal firewall that blocks unknown programs from answering the internet, and anti-virus that knows the trojan, stop a remote-control tool before a stranger can use it. Keep nothing secret in plain files on the desktop.
